                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.11/1235: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-08-02T17:00:00+08:00">August 2, 1930—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 3—11:10 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>653. With reference to telegrams from the American Consul at Hankow transmitted
                    to the Commander in Chief, the latter has replied as follows July 31st: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“There are now 3 American gunboats in the vicinity of Hankow. The U.S.
                            Steamship <hi rendition="#italic">Luzon</hi> is leaving Changsha, August
                            1st. In my opinion there is appropriate naval protection for Hankow and
                            the difference [<hi rendition="#italic">difficulty?</hi>] is due to the
                            failure of nationals to seek a place of safety and remain away from
                            unsafe places after repeated instructions from the Legation and consular
                            authorities to do so. There are also reported 10 foreign men-of-war in
                            the vicinity of Hankow and vessels of all navies are acting in
                            concert.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
